      "text": "First, we start with the congruence \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\). We need to find the modular inverse of 5 modulo 7. We know that \\(5 \\times -3 = -15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\), so the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is -3, which is equivalent to 4 modulo 7 (since \\(-3 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}\\)). Thus,\n\n\\[\nc \\equiv 2 \\cdot 4 \\pmod{7} \\implies c \\equiv 8 \\pmod{7} \\implies c \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the second equation \\(6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}\\). Rearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n6b - b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies 5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nAgain, we use the fact that 5 has an inverse of 3 modulo 7 (since \\(5 \\cdot 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\)), so we can multiply both sides by 3 to solve for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nb \\equiv 3 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 9 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we know \\(b = 2\\) and \\(c = 1\\). Next, we substitute these into the first equation \\(abc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\):\n\n\\[\na \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 2a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nWe need the inverse of 2 modulo 7. Since \\(2 \\cdot 4 = 8 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\), the inverse of 2 modulo 7 is 4. Thus,\n\n\\[\na \\equiv 1 \\cdot 4 \\pmod{7} \\implies a \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have \\(a = 4\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = 1\\). The value of \\(a + b + c\\) is:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7.\n\\]\n\nFinally, the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7 is:\n\n\\[\n7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
