      "text": "To solve for \\(a, b, c\\) that satisfy the given congruences, we will analyze each equation step by step.\n\nFirst, consider the congruence \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\). We need to find \\(c\\) such that when multiplied by 5 and taken modulo 7, the result is 2. We can test values of \\(c\\) from 1 to 6:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n5 \\cdot 1 &\\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &\\equiv 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &\\equiv 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4 &\\equiv 20 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 5 &\\equiv 25 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 6 &\\equiv 30 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\) is satisfied when \\(c = 6\\).\n\nNext, consider the congruence \\(6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}\\). Simplify this to:\n\n\\[\n6b - b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies 5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(b\\) such that when multiplied by 5 and taken modulo 7, the result is 3. We can test values of \\(b\\) from 1 to 6:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n5 \\cdot 1 &\\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &\\equiv 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &\\equiv 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4 &\\equiv 20 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 5 &\\equiv 25 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 6 &\\equiv 30 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\) is satisfied when \\(b = 2\\).\n\nFinally, substitute \\(b = 2\\) and \\(c = 6\\) into the first congruence \\(abc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\):\n\n\\[\na \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 12a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(12 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\nWe find \\(a\\) such that \\(5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\). From our previous results, we know that \\(5 \\cdot 2 = 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\), but this does not match 1. Instead, we need to find the modular inverse of 5 modulo 7. The inverse is an integer \\(x\\) such that:\n\n\\[\n5x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\nBy testing values, we find \\(5 \\cdot 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\), so the modular inverse is 3. Therefore, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nThus, \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = 6\\). The sum \\(a + b + c\\) is:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + 2 + 6 = 11\n\\]\n\nFinally, we need the remainder when this sum is divided by 7:\n\n\\[\n11 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7 is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
